        "<p>If the mass of the degenerate core exceeds the maximum limit supported by electron degeneracy, the core undergoes catastrophic collapse into a neutron star or a black hole, where quantum gravitational effects become prominent. In the extreme curved spacetime surrounding these black holes, quantum field theory predicts the steady emission of thermal particles, a quantum evaporation process known as <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/hawking-radiation\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Hawking Radiation</strong></a>. This radiative loss of mass causes the black hole to slowly shrink and ultimately explode, showing how quantum mechanics and general relativity interact at the horizon boundary. By linking this quantum evaporation to stellar collapse, the theory provides a unified model of compact object lifecycles.</p>"
        "<p>The quantitative mathematical engine of this degenerate mass limit is formulated by the Chandrasekhar relation. This fundamental physical formula maps the maximum stable mass directly to the fundamental constants of quantum mechanics and gravity:</p>"
        "<div class=\"math-display\" style=\"text-align: center; margin: 25px 0;\">\\[ M_{\\text{Ch}} \\approx \\left(\\frac{\\hbar c}{G m_p^2}\\right)^{3/2} m_p \\]</div>"
        "<p>This algebraic equation defines the Chandrasekhar mass limit \\( M_{\\text{Ch}} \\) as a function of the reduced Planck constant \\( \\hbar \\), the speed of light \\( c \\), the gravitational constant \\( G \\), and the proton mass \\( m_p \\), establishing the maximum mass of a stable white dwarf. The macroscopic limiting case of this stability threshold occurs in the limit of exceptionally small gravitational coupling, or as the gravitational constant \\( G \\to 0 \\). Under this zero-gravity limit, the calculated Chandrasekhar mass limit diverges smoothly to infinity, and the mechanical stability boundary disappears completely. Consequently, the highly non-local and quantum-constrained features of degenerate stars contract to the standard flat-space limit of an unconstrained electron gas, where the star can support any mass without undergoing collapse. This transition mathematically guarantees that classical flat-space physics is recovered without loss of consistency, ensuring that degenerate stellar models remain compatible with flat-space quantum statistics.</p>"
