        "<p>On cosmological scales, the global curvature parameter \\( k \\) of the universe determines the expansion history and the rate of structure formation. In the history of the expanding manifold with scale factor \\( a(t) \\), the expansion rate was dominated by the gravitational pull of non-relativistic matter during <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/matter-domination\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>The Matter-Dominated Era</strong></a>. During this epoch, the density of matter \\( \\rho_m \\) scaled inversely with the cube of the scale factor \\( a \\), driving the growth of primordial density perturbations \\( \\delta \\rho / \\rho \\) that later collapsed to form galaxies and clusters. By measuring the redshifts and distances of these galaxies, cosmologists can map the expansion rate, showing how global spacetime curvature is shaped by the mass-energy content of the universe. This large-scale geometric evolution dictates the growth rate of cosmic structures, demonstrating that the global curvature \\( k \\) acts as a dynamic coordinator for the distribution of galactic systems across the cosmic web.</p>"
        "<p>The macroscopic limiting case of this relativistic spacetime curvature model occurs in the limit of exceptionally weak gravitational fields, or as the mass-energy density of the system approaches zero. As the mass-energy density \\( T_{\\mu\\nu} \\) approaches zero, the Einstein tensor \\( G_{\\mu\\nu} \\) contracts smoothly to zero, and the local spacetime metric simplifies to the flat Minkowski metric \\( \\eta_{\\mu\\nu} \\). Under this flat, mass-free limit, the non-linear Einstein field equations simplify directly to the flat, static equations of classical special relativity in a global Lorentz frame, where space and time are independent and absolute. Consequently, the highly non-local and curved-space features of general relativity dissolve into the flat-space regularities of Minkowski spacetime \\( \\mathbb{R}^{1,3} \\), proving that general relativity is compatible with special relativity. This transition mathematically guarantees that the classical flat-space limit is recovered without any coordinate singularities, preserving the metric consistency of spacetime across all physical scales. Under this continuous reduction, the non-linear field equations contract into classical Newtonian potential balances \\( \\nabla^2 \\Phi = 4\\pi G \\rho \\) that govern local laboratory gravity.</p>"
