        "<p>During the late stages of stellar evolution, the nuclear processes in the core transition to heavy element synthesis, where neutrons are captured by seed nuclei to form heavier isotopes. This nucleosynthesis process, known as <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/neutron-capture\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Neutron Capture Nucleosynthesis</strong></a>, dictates the chemical composition of the star and the surrounding interstellar medium. The rates of these reactions are highly sensitive to the temperature and density profiles predicted by the modified Poisson equation. On cosmological scales, the expansion rate of the universe is described by the density parameters of matter and dark energy, which are parameterized by the cosmological <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/expansion-parameter\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Expansion Parameters (\\(q\\) and \\(w\\))</strong></a>. By measuring these expansion parameters, cosmologists can determine the deceleration parameter and the dark energy equation of state, proving that cosmic acceleration is driven by a dark energy component. This global mapping ensures that the calculated expansion rates are consistent with the local distance indicators, showing how local gravitational relations and global cosmology are integrated.</p>"
